<context query="Implement and repair the usage aggregation hook contract for chat orchestrator tool rounds; follow repository traceability and testing conventions." template="implementation" budget="5000">
<section name="Conventions" source="repo" priority="1">
<document source="specs/hal-engine-config-hooks/spec.md" type="spec" relevance="1.00" tokens="863">
## Installing hooks through config

- A hook passed through `orchestrator.hooks` is forwarded to the orchestrator and fires ([validated by: forwards an orchestrator hook, so one passed through the config actually fires](../../src/config.test.ts#L27)).
- An engine built with no `orchestrator` key at all still processes a message, so the field is optional in fact and not only in the type ([validated by: processes a message when the config declares no orchestrator key at all](../../src/config.test.ts#L103)).
- A `beforeModelResponse` hook installed through config is handed the base system prompt built from `prompt`, proving the hook reaches the orchestrator and is wired into the prompt pipeline rather than only being stored on the config ([validated by: hands a config-installed beforeModelResponse hook the built base system prompt, not merely storing it](../../src/config.test.ts#L154)).
- The value the hook returns then replaces the system prompt the provider receives; this is exercised at the orchestrator layer because `HalEngineConfig.provider` takes a `ProviderConfig` and offers no seam to inject a recording provider through config ([validated by: replaces system prompt with returned value](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.test.ts#L246)).
- For a session that completes without error the hooks fire in the order `beforeSession` → `beforeUserInput` → `afterUserInput` → `beforeModelResponse` → `afterModelResponse` → `afterSession` ([validated by: fires the lifecycle hooks in documented order for a session that completes without error](../../src/config.test.ts#L188)).
- A `beforeSession` that throws reaches the caller as a rejection, and also runs `onError` with the thrown error and then `afterSession`, because the call sits inside the same `try`/`finally` as the rest of the turn. An `afterSession` hook called under this condition can infer the session never advanced past `beforeSession`: no user-input hooks ran, no model response was generated, and no tool rounds were started — a cleanup hook that allocates in `beforeSession` and frees in `afterSession` will therefore always see the allocation and the release on the same error path ([validated by: runs onError then afterSession when it throws, and still rejects to the caller](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.test.ts#L141)).
- `afterModelResponse` is called once per user message after the round loop exits and is passed `totalUsage` — the sum of the usage every provider call in the turn reported, not the last round alone ([validated by: sums the usage of every round, not only the last](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.test.ts#L616)).
- When no provider call in the turn reports usage, `afterModelResponse` is passed `undefined`, not a zeroed total ([validated by: hands afterModelResponse undefined, not a zeroed total, when no round of the turn reports usage](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.test.ts#L661)).
- Every rejection reaches `onError`: a value that is not already an `Error` is wrapped in one whose `cause` is that original value, so a hook typed against `Error` still fires and a hook that wants the raw value can still reach it, while the caller receives the original rejection unchanged ([validated by: wraps a non-Error rejection in an Error whose cause is the original and still rejects the caller with the original](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.test.ts#L336)).

# Config-Installed Orchestrator Hooks
</document>
<document source="specs/hal-engine-architecture/spec.md" type="spec" relevance="0.98" tokens="841" truncated="true">
## Tool Execution Loop

At the start of every turn, after the input hooks and before `beforeModelResponse`, the orchestrator reads the `ToolRegistry` once. That read gives it the definitions it sends the provider and the `promptInstructions` of each tool, which `PromptBuilder` assembles alongside the AI's identity, domain context, and response guidelines into the system prompt for that turn.

### What a turn reads from the registry

- A tool registered after the orchestrator was built is sent to the provider and named in the system prompt, under the default tool preamble, from the next turn, so a registry that starts empty may be filled after construction ([validated by: sends weather, registered after the orchestrator was built on an empty registry, as the only tool and names it under the default preamble](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L48)).
- A tool registered between two turns is absent from the first turn's request and present in the second's ([validated by: sends tools undefined on the first turn and weather alone on the second when weather is registered between them](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L65)).
- The read is per turn, not per round: a tool registered while a turn runs, by one of its executors say, reaches no later round of that turn, so the list the model sees does not change while an unanswered `tool_use` block from an earlier round is still in its messages ([validated by: keeps weather, registered by an executor mid-turn, out of round 2 of that turn and sends it from the next turn](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L77)).
- A registry with no tools sends the provider `tools: undefined`, never an empty array, because Bedrock and Vertex read an empty list differently; Bedrock's request then carries no `toolConfig` key ([validated by: calls sendMessage with tools undefined, and Bedrock builds a request with no toolConfig key](../../src/emptyToolList.test.ts#L28)).
- A definition registered as a thunk is read once at the start of each turn for what that turn advertises, so a definition it changes between turns reaches the provider changed ([validated by: invokes a definition thunk once in each of two turns that call no tool, so the second turn sends its changed description](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L99)).
- Each call the turn executes reads the thunk once more, to validate that call's input, so a turn calling the tool once invokes it twice ([validated by: invokes a definition thunk twice in a turn that calls its tool once: at the turn start, then for the call](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L124)).
- A hook sees the same read: `beforeModelResponse` receives a prompt naming a tool registered after `createHalEngine` returned ([validated by: hands beforeModelResponse a prompt naming weather, a tool registered after createHalEngine returned](../../src/config.test.ts#L167)).
- Every tool `description`, `inputSchema` and `promptInstructions` string is sent to the model provider every turn, not once per process, so under GDPR a definition carries no personal data and no internal hostname; the orchestrator logs a tool's name and never its definition ([validated by: writes no tool description, input schema or prompt instructions to the log over a turn that calls the tool](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L142)).
</document>
</section>
